markdown <<MARKDOWN
# Validating and Analyzing Data

#### Before working with data, it's good practice to validate it.

<div style="background-color: #B7E0E5; border-radius: 10px; padding: 15px; margin: 20px auto; max-width: 85%; box-sizing: border-box; word-wrap: break-word; overflow-wrap: break-word;">
<p style="font-size: 14pt; margin-top: 12px; line-height: 120%;">📖 <strong>Validating Data</strong> means to check for misleading or fabricated data (e.g. duplicate entries, mismatched authors, or impossible dates).</p>
</div>

#### Analyzing data is a crucial step in making sense of information and drawing meaningful insights. Computers use various techniques to organize and interpret data efficiently. 

<div style="background-color: #F5F7F8; color: #000000; padding: 15px; border-radius: 10px; width: 90%; margin: 20px auto;">
<p style = "font-size:14pt;"><strong>Ways to Analyze Data:</strong></p>
  <ul style = "font-size:14pt;">
     <li><strong>Sorting</strong> – Organizing data in a specific order. <br>For example, sorting names alphabetically, ranking scores.</li><br>
    <li><strong>Filtering</strong> – Selecting only relevant data based on conditions. <br>For example, showing only sales over 0, finding students with A grades.</li><br>
    <li><strong>Grouping</strong> – Categorizing similar data points together. <br>For example, grouping sales by region, organizing students by grade level.</li><br>
    <li><strong>Comparing</strong> – Examining relationships between data points. <br>For example, comparing monthly sales, analyzing test scores across schools.</li><br>
    <li><strong>Aggregating</strong> – Summarizing data into key metrics. <br>For example,  calculating averages, finding total revenue, counting customer transactions.</li>
</ul>
</div>
MARKDOWN
